    ],
    "exampleHtml": "Be careful! You'll <span class=\"vp-verb\">hurt</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">yourself</span>. · Amira <span class=\"vp-verb\">cut</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">herself</span>. · They <span class=\"vp-verb\">help</span> <span class=\"vp-prep\">each other</span>.",
    "qa": [
      {
        "html": "Use a <strong>reflexive pronoun</strong> when the subject and the object are the <strong>same person</strong>. <em>I cut the bread.</em> (the bread is something else) · <em>I cut myself.</em> (I am the one who is cut)",
        "tag": "Same person"
      },
      {
        "html": "The pronouns end in <strong>-self</strong> (one person) or <strong>-selves</strong> (more than one): <em>myself, yourself, himself, herself, itself, ourselves, yourselves, themselves</em>. Not <s>hisself</s>, <s>theirselves</s> or <s>themself</s>.",
        "tag": "The forms"
      },
      {
        "html": "Some phrases are very common: <em>hurt yourself, cut yourself, enjoy yourself, introduce yourself, teach yourself, look at yourself</em>. <em>Help yourself</em> means \"take what you want\": <em>Help yourself to a biscuit.</em>",
        "tag": "Common phrases"
      },
      {
        "html": "<strong>by + -self</strong> means <em>alone</em> or <em>without help</em>: <em>I live by myself. She did it all by herself.</em>",
        "tag": "by myself"
      },
      {
        "html": "Many verbs are <strong>not reflexive in English</strong>, even if they are in your language. We say <em>I feel tired</em>, <em>We meet at eight</em>, <em>Relax!</em>, <em>Hurry up!</em>. For washing we usually say <em>I have a shower</em> or <em>I wash my hands</em>.",
        "tag": "No -self"
      },
      {
        "html": "Use <strong>each other</strong> when two (or more) people do the same thing to one another: <em>Amira and Tom help each other.</em> (Amira helps Tom, and Tom helps Amira.) Compare: <em>They hurt themselves.</em> (each person hurt their own body)",
        "tag": "each other"
      },
      {
        "html": "Typical mistakes: ✗ <em>I feel myself tired.</em> → ✓ <em>I feel tired.</em> · ✗ <em>He hurt hisself.</em> / <em>They hurt theirselves.</em> → ✓ <em>He hurt himself. / They hurt themselves.</em> · ✗ <em>Liam and Mei love themselves.</em> (when you mean one another) → ✓ <em>Liam and Mei love each other.</em>",
        "tag": "Typical mistakes"
      }
    ],
    "noteHtml": "With parts of the body, use a possessive word, not a reflexive pronoun: <em>I washed my hands.</em> (not <s>I washed myself the hands</s>). See <a href=\"/possessives\">Possessive adjectives</a>. You can also use <em>-self</em> to stress who did something: <em>I made this cake myself.</em> The subject pronouns (<em>I, you, he, she …</em>) are in <a href=\"/pronouns-and-be\">Subject pronouns and the verb be</a>.",
